A||||7||Black86SE||Recall Problems||||1||
Z||000000||Black86SE||10-04-2001||04:17 PM||||I currently have my car into a dealership to get my fire recall done. They said it would be done by 5:00 tonight. Well, I got a phone call from them around 3:30 and they said that they "ran into a problem." I thought great. They said that my engine was missing a couple of pieces. He told me that I was missing the EGR tube (which was missing when I bought it,) and if I had it to bring it in. I told him that it didn't come with it and I had made one but haven't had a chance to put it on yet. The rear manifold had a chunk of steel missing where one of the bolts entered the block (you could see the bolt). Anyway, aparently this prevents them from performing the recall until they find out if GM will allow it. They are gonna call GM to find out, tomorrow! It was only 3:30, why not call today?! I might go tonight and pick up the car and say, "When you find out if you can do it, call me. Until then, bye." <P>What difference does it make that those parts are gone? They don't impact the parts they install for the recall do they? Makes me mad.<P><BR>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<p>[This message has been edited by Black86SE (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||10-04-2001||04:55 PM||||<BR>Well, the Exhaust Gas Recirculation (EGR) tube is for emissions, and legally GM is not allowed to have any Fiero serviced with out that item, this is an calculated assumption based on legalities. Due to the emmissions rules from EPA, so they could get in big trouble, hence why the dealership is going to call and verify the situation.<P>As for the chunk of steel missing, I think it would depend on to the extent that it affects the exhaust manifold's operation.  If the missing piece does not affect the operation of the manifold I don't see what they would not allow it as is.  However, if the missing steel is resulting in exhaust leaks then it should be fixed, imo.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black86SE||10-04-2001||06:54 PM||||Yeah, I can understand the ERG tube problem. I didn't want to buy one if I didn't have to but I might do it anyway if my homemade one doesn't work. As far as the manifold goes, the bolt is still holding the manifold tight against the block. Unless the gasket is bad I would think that it is still okay. Otherwise, how would I go about fixing it? Or would I have to install a new one?<P>I might try to get a pic of it when I get it back from the dealer.<P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-04-2001||08:11 PM||||Best bet is to replace the exhast manifold.  A crack you can weld back together, a chunk missing.....||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||10-04-2001||09:33 PM||||It's difficult enough to get a recall done. If they've agreed to do it, you've half the battle won. Change the exh and replace the tube. Otherwise, it leaves them a way out of doing the recall.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black86SE||10-04-2001||10:22 PM||||I found out from another friend that they shouldn't even have to do anything with that manifold. The one that they have to put the heat shield on is the one by the firewall. The chunk missing one is by the trunk. That should not hinder their ability to do the recall. <P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image2_4.gif"> <BR>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<p>[This message has been edited by Black86SE (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||OcalaFiero||10-05-2001||08:20 AM||||Yeah, it doesn't affect the recall work, but as Gold-86SE said, EPA laws may prohibit them from sending the car out that way. It may end up being an "all or nothing" deal as far as the dealer is concerned.<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>Ocala, Florida<BR>'86 2M4<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallsigpic_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black86SE||10-06-2001||04:54 PM||||Well, good news. They agreed to do the recall after all. PLUS, they are replacing like 4 or 6 other gaskets that were leaking free of charge. But they said that they have to make note of and tell me that because the car has no catalytic converter, it shouldn't be driven, but towed to the nearest muffler shop. Or they can put one on and the converter will cost, get this, $666.25. Just for the converter. HA! Oh well, it is gonna be picked up on monday. Which is good since my truck is leaking tranny fluid again. Grrr.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image2_4.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
